#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fba8401-2da1-50f2-8e96-84cbe06d95fc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Dot Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c374456a-bff7-5145-a590-620bbf50dabf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5536" :question/problem "Find $(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d}$, if $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 2$, $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} = -1$, $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 2$, and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}=3$." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5a + 3b) \\cdot c + (4b + 2c) \\cdot d &= (5a) \\cdot c + (3b) \\cdot c + (4b) \\cdot d + (2c) \\cdot d \\\\\n&= 5(a \\cdot c) + 3(b \\cdot c) + 4(b \\cdot d) + 2(c \\cdot d)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can substitute the values for $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d}$, and use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(a \\cdot c) + 3(b \\cdot c) + 4(b \\cdot d) + 2(c \\cdot d) &= 5(c \\cdot a) + 3(-1) + 4(2) + 2(d \\cdot c) \\\\\n&= 5(c \\cdot a) - 3 + 8 + 2(d \\cdot c) \\\\\n&= 5(c \\cdot a) + 5 + 2(d \\cdot c)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}$, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(c \\cdot a) + 5 + 2(d \\cdot c) &= 5(2) + 5 + 2(3) \\\\\n&= 10 + 5 + 6 \\\\\n&= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 21\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133488 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/id #uuid "13909d3e-b3df-5fcb-9056-5d71283fbebe" :question/math-academy-id "e-5537" :question/problem "Suppose $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0}$, where $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = -2(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a})$\n2. $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 0$\n3. $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Simplifying, we have\n$(4a + 2b) \\cdot a|= 0; (4a) \\cdot a + (2b) \\cdot a|= 0; 4(a \\cdot a) + 2(b \\cdot a)|= 0; 2(b \\cdot a)|=-4(a \\cdot a); b \\cdot a|=-2(a \\cdot a)$.\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$, we must have $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0$.\n- Statement III is false since it leads to a contradiction. Simplifying $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0}$ and substituting in $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1$, we get\n$(4a + 2b) \\cdot a|= 0; 4a \\cdot a + 2b \\cdot a|= 0; 4a \\cdot a + 2(1)|= 0; a \\cdot a|=-\\frac{1}{2}$,\nbut this is a contradiction since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ implies that $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98eb5973-f9b1-5c2f-8763-e8f8707e9b89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Dot Product"}] :question/id #uuid "7000f188-3223-5a2c-afe6-45fd3f92b8c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5907" :question/problem "If $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 34$, then what is $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}}$?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}$, we compute $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ first and then multiply the result by $2$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot 2v &= 2(u \\cdot v) \\\\\n&= 2(u_{1} \\cdot v_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot v_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot v_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot v_{4}) \\\\\n&= 2(7 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 2 + (-9) \\cdot (-5) + 3 \\cdot (-2)) \\\\\n&= 2(7 + 10 + 45 - 6) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 56 \\\\\n&= 112\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $3\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$, we multiply $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ by $3$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3v \\cdot v &= 3(v \\cdot v) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 34 \\\\\n&= 102\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}} =\\dfrac{112}{102}=\\dfrac{56}{51}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fba8401-2da1-50f2-8e96-84cbe06d95fc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac80673b-411c-5163-b208-8bf7d3f3cc03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131175} {:answer/id #uuid "11e8f4f3-3eee-539f-be28-2951832419da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131176} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad8ac6d-1ec4-5ae1-a4f3-3ca0cf31eafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131177} {:answer/id #uuid "56dffa35-c327-550b-b4ed-4bcd0acb1cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131178} {:answer/id #uuid "644ea17e-d548-59b3-a221-3676f4c3376e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ad8ac6d-1ec4-5ae1-a4f3-3ca0cf31eafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131177} :answer-field/id #uuid "d159b7ba-de36-5978-83a2-e9dff9d080e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131174}] :question/id #uuid "85c8d44f-ab83-5536-bc23-d67925596d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-39202" :question/problem "Find $(3a + b) \\cdot c + (2b - 2c) \\cdot d$, if $c \\cdot a = 3$, $c \\cdot b =-2$, $b \\cdot d =-1$, and $d \\cdot c = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98eb5973-f9b1-5c2f-8763-e8f8707e9b89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1b4eb15-da66-59d8-ba2f-7c923bb902e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131187} {:answer/id #uuid "c3127ec9-d1ca-531c-8b7e-1f52ad89c001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131188} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5642a4-92b2-5082-974e-cba121993fb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131189} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba49700-af69-523d-afe7-a9462360a76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131190} {:answer/id #uuid "74566f91-cd06-507e-a39c-8a9f7b1c25aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3127ec9-d1ca-531c-8b7e-1f52ad89c001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131188} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f95a03-5a4d-5fe8-a222-3f69319730f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131186}] :question/id #uuid "687f42ad-4d31-5616-a543-616339685f35" :question/math-academy-id "q-39205" :question/problem "If $u = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$, and $u \\cdot u = 15$, then $(u \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u))/(u \\cdot (\\fr